  "teaser": "The jazz musicians who traded the concert hall for the dancefloor — Norman Connors to Grover Washington, an hour of crossings that stuck.",
  "description": "This one is about the musicians who could play anything and chose to play for the heart and the hips. Norman Connors behind a kit that opened into something warmer. Donny Hathaway at the electric piano, Howard and the church talking to each other in every chord. Michael Henderson stepping away from Miles Davis's bass line and up to the microphone.\n\nMid-show, a question about where to start with Roy Ayers. The vibraphone holds you the way a warm light left on in a window does. My answer is Everybody Loves the Sunshine.\n\nThe second half follows the thread outward: Roy as producer, Miles sidemen producing Phyllis Hyman, George Benson walking jazz guitar onto the dancefloor, and finally Grover Washington with Grady Tate — a jazz drummer closing the circle.\n\n— Simone",
  "quote": "The vibraphone is one of those instruments that shouldn't work the way it does. It's percussion — metal bars and spinning resonators — but in Roy Ayers' hands it sounds like something between a voice and a warm light left on in a window.",
  "home_quote": {
    "text": "Jazz finesse and dancefloor pull, *sitting together without a word of argument between them.*",
    "context": "On George Benson, Off Broadway"
  },
